WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.960
Dynamic Application Security Testing, or DAST, evaluates a running application from the outside.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:18.180
Instead of reading source code directly, a DAST process interacts with deployed behavior: pages, forms, APIs, headers, cookies, errors, sessions, redirects, and responses.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:30.480
That external perspective is valuable because it shows how the application behaves when requests actually reach the runtime environment, configuration, middleware, authentication layer, and supporting services.

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:32.860
DAST is different from related practices.

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:36.680
SAST analyzes source code or artifacts before runtime.

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:40.180
IAST observes behavior during execution with instrumentation.

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:43.120
SCA focuses on third-party component risk.

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:46.060
Secrets scanning searches for exposed credentials.

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:51.480
Manual testing and penetration testing add human judgment and broader scenario exploration.

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:57.700
DAST complements these practices by producing runtime evidence from the application’s observable surface.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:11.520
Common DAST finding categories include injection indicators, authentication weaknesses, session handling issues, security header gaps, misconfiguration, exposed errors, insecure transport, and access control concerns.

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:15.580
A finding does not automatically prove a full exploit path.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:24.600
It indicates that runtime behavior should be reviewed, understood, reproduced safely, and either remediated, accepted, or dismissed with evidence.

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:28.980
DAST is a defensive testing practice, not a complete security guarantee.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:35.120
A scanner can only evaluate what it reaches, understands, and is authorized to test.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:42.220
It may miss logic flaws, role-specific paths, business rules, or issues hidden behind complex workflows.

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:50.200
Used well, DAST gives teams a repeatable way to catch observable security problems before attackers, customers, or auditors encounter them.
